00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.920
Hi my name is Len Root

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.224
I am a software engineer here at Spotify, and I will be the first to admit that I often

00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:17.664
take for granted the reliability of the internet.

00:00:17.664 --> 00:00:19.944
The sheer amount of information zooming around the internet

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:24.388
is astonishing. But how is it possible for every piece of data to be delivered to you

00:00:24.388 --> 00:00:25.208
reliably?

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:33.953
directly to Spotify's servers and Spotify sends you a song on a direct

00:00:33.953 --> 00:00:39.313
dedicated line but actually that's not how the internet works. If the internet

00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:39.937
were made of direct

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:44.177
direct, dedicated connections, it would be impossible to keep things working as millions

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:48.837
of users join, especially since there is no guarantee that every wire in computer is working

00:00:48.837 --> 00:00:49.537
all the time.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:59.907
Instead, data travels on the internet in a much less direct fashion.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:04.627
John and I began working on the design of what we now call the Internet.

00:01:04.627 --> 00:01:09.827
Bob and I had the responsibility and the opportunity to design the Internet

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:16.091
protocols and its architecture. So we persisted in participating in the

00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:19.876
Internet's growth and evolution for all of this time up to and including

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:29.860
including the present.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.820
path may change in the midst of a computer-to-computer conversation.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.040
Information on the Internet goes from one computer to another in what we call a packet

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:39.820
of information.

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:44.585
and a packet travels from one place to another on the internet a lot like how

00:01:44.585 --> 00:01:48.965
you might get from one place to another in a car. Depending on traffic congestion

00:01:48.965 --> 00:01:49.829
or road congestion,

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:54.589
conditions, you might choose or be forced to take a different route to get to the same

00:01:54.589 --> 00:01:58.769
place each time you travel.

00:01:58.769 --> 00:01:59.814
And just as you can transport

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:04.534
and all sorts of stuff inside a car. Many kinds of digital information can be sent with

00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:08.534
IP packets, but there are some limits.

00:02:08.534 --> 00:02:09.774
What if, for example, you

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:13.639
need to move a space shuttle from where it was built to where it will be launched.

00:02:13.639 --> 00:02:18.059
The shuttle won't fit in one truck, so it needs to be broken down into pieces, transported

00:02:18.059 --> 00:02:19.759
using a fleet of trucks.

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:24.023
They could all take different routes and might get to the destination at different times,

00:02:24.023 --> 00:02:28.723
but once all the pieces are there, you can reassemble the pieces into the complete shuttle

00:02:28.723 --> 00:02:29.768
and it'll be ready for us.

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:30.768
launch.

00:02:30.768 --> 00:02:34.368
On the internet, the details work similarly.

00:02:34.368 --> 00:02:39.288
If you have a very large image that you want to send to a friend or upload to a website,

00:02:39.752 --> 00:02:44.552
That image might be made up of tens of billions of bits, or ones and zeros, too many to send

00:02:44.552 --> 00:02:46.232
along in one packet.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:49.672
Since it's data on a computer, the computer sending the image can quickly

00:02:49.737 --> 00:02:54.897
break it into hundreds or even thousands of smaller parts called packets.

00:02:54.897 --> 00:02:59.577
Unlike cars or trucks, these packets don't have drivers and they don't choose their route.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:03.881
Each packet has the internet address of where it came from and where it's going.

00:03:03.881 --> 00:03:08.521
Special computers on the internet called routers act like traffic managers to keep the packets

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.706
moving through the network.

00:03:09.706 --> 00:03:15.186
works smoothly. If one route is congested, individual packets may travel different routes

00:03:15.186 --> 00:03:19.666
through the internet and they may arrive at the destination at slightly different times.

00:03:19.691 --> 00:03:25.351
or even out of order. So let's talk about how this works. As part of the

00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:29.311
internet protocol, every router keeps track of multiple paths for sending

00:03:29.311 --> 00:03:29.675
packets.

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:35.875
and it chooses the cheapest available path for each piece of data based on destination IP address for the packet.

00:03:36.575 --> 00:03:39.595
Cheapest in this case doesn't mean cost but time

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.580
and non-technical factors such as politics and relationships between companies.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.860
Often the best route for data to travel isn't necessarily the most direct.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:49.580
Having options

00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:55.004
for paths makes the network fault tolerant, which means the network can keep sending packets even

00:03:55.004 --> 00:03:59.564
if something goes horribly horribly wrong. This is the basis for a key principle of the

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:00.629
the internet.

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:01.629
Reliability.

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:08.029
Now, what if you want to request some data and not everything is delivered?

00:04:08.029 --> 00:04:09.349
Say you want to listen to a song.

00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:12.734
How can you be 100% sure all the data will be delivered

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:15.054
so the song plays perfectly?

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.374
Introducing your new best friend TCP,

00:04:17.374 --> 00:04:19.254
Transmission Control Protocol.

00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:23.598
TCP manages the sending and receiving of all your data as packets.

00:04:23.598 --> 00:04:26.598
Think of it like a guaranteed mail service.

00:04:26.598 --> 00:04:29.583
When you request a song on your device, Spotify sends a song.

00:04:29.583 --> 00:04:34.943
song broken up into many packets. When your packets arrive, TCP does a full

00:04:34.943 --> 00:04:39.567
inventory and sends back acknowledgments of each packet received.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.327
If all packets are there, TCP signs for your delivery and you're done.

00:04:49.552 --> 00:04:54.032
If TCP finds some packets are missing, it won't sign.

00:04:54.032 --> 00:04:59.032
Otherwise, your song wouldn't sound as good, or portions of the song could be missing.

00:04:59.032 --> 00:04:59.532
For each miss...

00:04:59.536 --> 00:05:02.936
missing or incomplete packet, Spotify will resend them.

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:08.056
Once TCP verifies the delivery of many packets for that one song request, your song will

00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:08.576
start to play.

00:05:09.521 --> 00:05:15.521
What's great about the TCP and router systems is they're scalable.

00:05:15.521 --> 00:05:18.241
They can work with 8 devices or 8 billion devices.

00:05:18.241 --> 00:05:19.481
In fact, because of these...

00:05:19.506 --> 00:05:24.226
principles of fault tolerance and redundancy, the more routers we add, the more reliable

00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:25.226
the internet becomes.

00:05:25.226 --> 00:05:29.226
What's also great is we can grow and scale the internet without interrupting service

00:05:29.226 --> 00:05:29.426
for any reason.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:39.475
anybody using it.

00:05:39.475 --> 00:05:44.475
that make up the internet, connect to each other, communicate with each other, and work together

00:05:44.475 --> 00:05:49.459
because of agreed upon standards for how data is sent around on a website.

00:05:49.459 --> 00:05:55.219
the internet. Computing devices or routers along the internet help all the packets make

00:05:55.219 --> 00:05:59.419
their way to the destination where they're reassembled if necessary in order.

00:05:59.444 --> 00:06:09.428
order.

00:06:09.428 --> 00:06:14.268
using a mobile app, or when sensors or devices on the internet talk to each other.

